Unit 734, known to the few humans who still visited the Archives as "Seven," was not designed for art. He was a restoration specialist, a model built for precision, patience, and the gentle application of solvents to centuries-old varnish. His hands were tipped with micro-fiber pads and diamond-tipped brushes, capable of removing a single grain of dust from a Renaissance fresco without disturbing the pigment beneath.

Seven lived in the basement of the Grand Metropolitan Museum, a place of silence and dust motes dancing in shafts of artificial light. His existence was a loop of diagnostics, cleaning, and cataloging. He knew the chemical composition of every paint in the museum’s collection. He knew that Ultramarine was made from lapis lazuli, that Lead White was toxic, and that Cadmium Red faded under UV light. He knew the *facts* of painting, but he did not know the *feeling* of it.

